1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,535
Reactor gave us Mono, Flux, schedulers,
and backpressure.

2
00:00:04,655 --> 00:00:15,047
Spring WebFlux is how those types become
HTTP servers and clients on a non-blocking
stack — typically Netty with Boot’s
WebFlux starter, not an embedded Tomcat
servlet container.

3
00:00:15,167 --> 00:00:27,287
For the harbor ops board, that means a
reactive /positions/stream endpoint that
writes AIS fixes as they arrive instead of
parking a servlet thread per open map.
Contrast the center of gravity.

4
00:00:27,407 --> 00:00:34,943
Spring MVC: DispatcherServlet, thread-per-
request style, blocking signatures
returning objects or ResponseEntity.

5
00:00:35,064 --> 00:00:47,567
WebFlux: reactive HTTP, handlers that
return Mono / Flux, and a choice of
programming model — annotated
@RestController familiar from MVC, or
functional RouterFunction /
HandlerFunction beans.

6
00:00:47,688 --> 00:01:00,432
Same application for JSON APIs; different
concurrency contract. This is not servlet
DI with a new annotation coat — it is
publishers on the wire. Look at this java
for a moment.

7
00:01:00,551 --> 00:01:09,936
WebClient replaces blocking RestTemplate
in this world. It returns publishers; you
compose them with the same operators you
already learned.

8
00:01:10,055 --> 00:01:20,567
Connecting WebFlux to a blocking
DataSource without offloading will stall
Netty event loops — pair WebFlux with
R2DBC or wrap blocking JDBC on
boundedElastic at a clear boundary.

9
00:01:20,688 --> 00:01:36,623
Boot chooses the stack from the classpath.
spring-boot-starter-web pulls MVC. spring-
boot-starter-webflux pulls WebFlux.
Putting both on the classpath makes Boot
prefer MVC unless you force a reactive
application type.

10
00:01:36,743 --> 00:01:45,215
Be explicit in multi-module builds so you
do not accidentally ship a servlet stack
while writing Mono return types that never
get a reactive runtime.

11
00:01:45,335 --> 00:01:56,831
Error handling and validation have
reactive-aware variants, but the teaching
point for this episode is the request
pipeline: channel reads bytes, decoding
produces objects, your handler returns a
publisher,

12
00:01:56,951 --> 00:02:02,447
encoding writes when items arrive,
cancellation propagates when clients
disconnect.

13
00:02:02,567 --> 00:02:10,056
That is why returning Flux for SSE on
/positions/stream is natural here and
awkward under a blocking servlet mindset.

14
00:02:10,175 --> 00:02:20,351
A misconception is “WebFlux is always
faster than MVC.” For modest concurrency
and blocking drivers, MVC is often simpler
and plenty fast.

15
00:02:20,471 --> 00:02:27,839
WebFlux shines when concurrency and I/O
wait dominate and the whole stack is non-
blocking — AIS fan-in is the motivating
shape.

16
00:02:27,959 --> 00:02:36,119
Another misconception is using WebFlux
only because it looks modern while calling
.block() in every handler — that is the
worst of both models.

17
00:02:36,239 --> 00:02:46,079
A third is treating WebFlux as a different
way to inject servlet beans rather than a
reactive HTTP runtime. We can serve
reactive HTTP.

18
00:02:46,199 --> 00:03:00,671
Security still matters — identity and
authorization cannot vanish because we
changed the server. The filter story must
move to the reactive channel with JWT on a
WebFlux filter chain. That is reactive
security.
